WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.740
Bath bars that are kind to skin and honest on the label.

2
00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:07.590
This is the video for Module 3: bath soap production.

3
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.320
A bath bar touches skin every day. It must clean without stripping the skin.

4
00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:15.830
It must lather well.

5
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.080
It must stay hard in the soap dish.

6
00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:25.030
And it must last. So this module is about balance, and about honest claims.

7
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:41.740
Melt-and-pour uses a soap base a factory has already made. You melt it gently, never boil it, add colour and scent, and pour. There is no caustic soda to handle, but each bar costs more. It suits gifts, samples and quick orders.

8
00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.250
Wrap bars straight away, or they sweat in humid air.

9
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:54.890
For your everyday bath line, a cold-process formula you calculate yourself is usually cheaper per bar and fully under your control.

10
00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:01.810
For example, Ifeoma in Enugu needs 10 guest bars of 100 g for a wedding.

11
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:17.500
The white base costs 7,500 naira. Fragrance at 2% is 20 g, costing 1,200 naira. Colour and alcohol are about 300 naira, and wraps and bands 1,000 naira.

12
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:27.030
That is 10,000 naira, so each bar costs her 1,000 naira before her time and profit. Now she can quote with confidence.

13
00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:37.050
For example, Zainab in Abuja designs a bar of 40% palm oil, 25% olive oil and 20% PKO.

14
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.750
Then 10% shea butter and 5% castor oil.

15
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:54.890
SoapCalc shows quality numbers for her oils. Cleansing should stay between 12 and 22. That usually means keeping PKO or coconut oil to about a fifth of the oils.

16
00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:06.830
For 2,000 g of oils at 5% superfat, she needs 275 g of pure caustic soda. That leaves more spare oil, for a milder bar.

17
00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:15.410
Her flakes are 99% pure, so she weighs 278 g, with 558 g of water.

18
00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:21.170
Mix the lye solution outdoors with full protective equipment, and let it cool.

19
00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:26.850
Melt the palm oil, PKO and shea, then add the olive and castor oil.

20
00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:36.210
When both are warm, not hot, pour the lye into the oils. Many makers combine below about 45 degrees Celsius.

21
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.150
Blend in short bursts until light trace, and add fragrance.

22
00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:47.500
Pour into a lined mould, cover it and wrap it in an old towel for 24 hours.

23
00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:59.010
Unmould and cut after one to two days, wearing gloves. Then cure the bars for 4 to 6 weeks in a shaded, airy room, and do a pH test before sale.

24
00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:09.090
Additives make a bar special, but start small and test. Kaolin clay starts at about 1 teaspoon per 500 g of oils.

25
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:16.360
Honey also starts at about 1 teaspoon. It adds heat, so do not wrap that mould in a towel.

26
00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:30.300
Fragrance is weighed from the oils. At 2%, 1,000 g of oils needs 20 g. Never go above the supplier's maximum, and leave out cinnamon and clove, which can irritate skin.

27
00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:36.990
A soap cleans. You may say it cleans gently, has a light scent, or contains shea butter.

28
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:48.050
You may not say it cures eczema, treats acne or removes dark spots. Medical claims make your soap a drug in the eyes of regulators such as NAFDAC.

29
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.550
Never add mercury, skin-bleaching chemicals or medicines, and never sell soap as a skin lightener.

30
00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:03.910
To remember. Melt-and-pour is quick but costs more per bar. Keep PKO to about a fifth, and use a 5% superfat.

31
00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.800
Cure for 4 to 6 weeks, and make only cosmetic claims.

32
00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:17.990
Now make and cost five sample bars, design and test your own bath bar in SoapCalc, and plan one speciality bar with an honest label.

33
00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.280
Continue with the lessons below.
